.\" fseek.3 - reposition a stream
.TH FSEEK 3 "May 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
fseek, ftell, rewind, fgetpos, fsetpos, fseeko, ftello \- reposition a stream
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "int fseek(FILE *" stream ", long " offset ", int " whence ");"
.PP
.BI "long ftell(FILE *" stream ");"
.PP
.BI "void rewind(FILE *" stream ");"
.PP
.BI "int fgetpos(FILE *" stream ", fpos_t *" pos ");"
.PP
.BI "int fsetpos(FILE *" stream ", const fpos_t *" pos ");"
.PP
.B #include <sys/types.h>
.PP
.BI "int fseeko(FILE *" stream ", off_t " offset ", int " whence ");"
.PP
.BI "off_t ftello(FILE *" stream ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR fseek ()
function sets the file position indicator for the stream pointed to by
.IR stream .
The new position, measured in bytes, is obtained by adding
.I offset
bytes to the position specified by
.IR whence .
The value of
.I whence
shall be one of:
.TP
.B SEEK_SET
The new file position is set to
.I offset
bytes from the beginning of the file.
.TP
.B SEEK_CUR
The new file position is set to its current location plus
.IR offset .
.TP
.B SEEK_END
The new file position is set to the size of the file plus
.IR offset .
.PP
Before the seek is performed,
.BR fseek ()
flushes any buffered output, invalidates the read buffer, discards a
character pushed back via
.BR ungetc (3),
and clears the end-of-file indicator for the stream. The seek itself is
implemented via
.BR lseek (2).
.PP
The
.BR fseeko ()
function is identical to
.BR fseek ()
except that the
.I offset
argument has type
.IR off_t ,
allowing positions outside the range of
.IR long .
.PP
The
.BR ftell ()
function obtains the current value of the file position indicator for
.IR stream .
For streams opened for reading the value returned by
.BR lseek (2)
is adjusted downward by the number of bytes that have been buffered but
not yet returned to the caller, including any character pending from
.BR ungetc (3).
For streams opened for writing the value is adjusted upward by the
number of bytes buffered but not yet flushed to the underlying file
descriptor.
.PP
The
.BR ftello ()
function is identical to
.BR ftell ()
except that the return type is
.IR off_t .
.PP
The
.BR rewind ()
function sets the file position indicator for
.I stream
to the beginning of the file. It is equivalent to:
.PP
.RS 4
(void) fseek(stream, 0L, SEEK_SET);
.RE
.PP
except that
.BR rewind ()
also clears the error indicator for the stream by calling
.BR clearerr (3).
.PP
The
.BR fgetpos ()
and
.BR fsetpos ()
functions are alternate interfaces equivalent to
.BR ftell ()
and
.BR fseek ()
(with
.I whence
set to
.BR SEEK_SET ),
setting and storing the current value of the file position indicator
through the object referenced by
.IR pos .
On Substrate
.I fpos_t
holds a byte offset compatible with
.IR "long" .
.SH RETURN VALUE
The
.BR fseek (),
.BR fseeko (),
and
.BR fsetpos ()
functions return 0 on success; otherwise, \-1 is returned and the error
indicator for the stream is set.
.PP
The
.BR ftell ()
and
.BR ftello ()
functions return the current offset, in bytes, from the beginning of
the file. On error, \-1 is returned.
.PP
The
.BR fgetpos ()
function returns 0 on success and \-1 on failure.
.PP
The
.BR rewind ()
function returns no value.
.SH ERRORS
These functions may fail with any of the errors specified for
.BR lseek (2),
including:
.TP
.B EBADF
The
.I stream
is not associated with a seekable file (for example, a pipe, FIFO, or
socket).
.TP
.B EINVAL
The
.I whence
argument is not
.BR SEEK_SET ,
.BR SEEK_CUR ,
or
.BR SEEK_END ,
or the resulting file offset would be negative.
.TP
.B EOVERFLOW
For
.BR ftell ()
and
.BR fgetpos (),
the resulting file offset cannot be represented in a
.IR long .
.PP
In addition, these functions may fail and set
.I errno
for any of the errors specified for the routines
.BR fflush (3)
and
.BR lseek (2).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lseek (2),
.BR clearerr (3),
.BR fflush (3),
.BR fopen (3),
.BR ungetc (3)
.SH STANDARDS
.BR fseek (),
.BR ftell (),
.BR rewind (),
.BR fgetpos (),
and
.BR fsetpos ()
conform to ISO C99.
.PP
.BR fseeko ()
and
.BR ftello ()
conform to POSIX.1-2001.
